<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Can anxiety cause physical pain every day?</h3>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Yes. Chronic anxiety sustains muscle tension, heightens pain sensitivity through central sensitization, and drives inflammation, all of which can produce daily physical pain. This is real pain — not imagined — and it requires treatment of the underlying anxiety to resolve.</p>

<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How long do physical anxiety symptoms last?</h3>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Acute physical symptoms triggered by a panic attack or acute anxiety may last minutes to an hour. Chronic physical symptoms driven by generalized anxiety can persist indefinitely until the anxiety is adequately treated. With effective therapy and/or medication, most physical symptoms improve significantly within weeks to months.</p>

<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Should I see a psychiatrist or a specialist for physical anxiety symptoms?</h3>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Start with your primary care physician to rule out medical causes. If medical workup is unremarkable and symptoms persist, a psychiatrist is the most appropriate specialist to evaluate for anxiety and coordinate a comprehensive treatment plan. <a href="/single-blog.php?slug=understanding-your-mental-health-the-vital-role-of-psychiatrists">The vital role psychiatrists play</a> in evaluating complex presentations like these is well-established.</p>

